### 5. API Response Compression

#### Current State
- No `compress` configuration in [next.config.js](file:///c:/Users/zaidi/Downloads/compliance-dev%20(4)/compliance-dev/next.config.js).
- Next.js 14 enables gzip compression by default (`compress: true`), but we should explicitly confirm this and add the `poweredByHeader: false` setting (also a minor security/perf improvement by removing the `X-Powered-By: Next.js` header).
- API responses (e.g., dashboard data, assessment checklists with 100+ controls) can be large JSON payloads that benefit significantly from compression.

#### [MODIFY] [next.config.js](file:///c:/Users/zaidi/Downloads/compliance-dev%20(4)/compliance-dev/next.config.js)

Add to `nextConfig`:
```javascript
compress: true,       // Explicit (default is true, but good to be intentional)
poweredByHeader: false, // Remove X-Powered-By header (less bytes + security)
```

#### [MODIFY] [api-helpers.ts](file:///c:/Users/zaidi/Downloads/compliance-dev%20(4)/compliance-dev/lib/api-helpers.ts)

Add `Cache-Control` headers to appropriate API responses:
- Dashboard data: `Cache-Control: private, max-age=30` (stale-while-revalidate pattern)
- Framework list: `Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, s-maxage=600` (frameworks change infrequently)
- Assessment data: `Cache-Control: private, no-cache` (always fresh)

This allows browser/CDN caching to reduce redundant API calls.

